<p>Among Beatles connoisseurs, it’s fair game to claim anything from <em>Rubber Soul</em> to <em>Abbey Road</em> as one’s favorite album. But even within that span of the catalog, a hierarchy of acclaim exists, and that hierarchy has been rearranged over the decades. The ecstatic reception of <em>Sgt. Pepper’s Lonely Hearts Club Band</em> lasted&nbsp;at least a decade. By the turn of the nineteen-eighties, its immediate predecessor <em>Revolver&nbsp;</em>had joined it at the top spot of critical regard;&nbsp;twenty years after that, <em>Abbey Road</em> had displaced&nbsp;<em>Sgt. Pepper</em>. Now, a full 60 years after it first came out,&nbsp;<em>Revolver </em>is the single Beatles album held in the highest esteem — which, naturally, makes it a&nbsp;serious contender for the&nbsp;greatest album ever recorded.</p>
<p>In <a href="https://youtu.be/YYFFKUpfqT8">his new video above</a>, music YouTuber David Hartley explains what makes <em>Revolver</em> so beloved by recounting the history of its production. As in his previous Beatles videos, he draws on the library of studio outtakes that make audible the evolution of each song, as well as on contemporary interviews with the Beatles and their collaborators.</p>
