</div>

<p>Among his most illuminating sources is engineer Geoff Emerick’s memoir <a href="https://amzn.to/4i30xjj"><em>Here, There, and Everywhere</em></a> — titled, incidentally after one of the songs on this very album, the challenge of whose&nbsp;recording he took on at just twenty years old. In the event, his youth proved an asset, granting him as it did the willingness not just to execute the Beatles’ “far-out” sonic ideas, but also to&nbsp;violate EMI Studios’ policies against&nbsp;experimental use of its hardware in the process.</p>
<p>Indeed, if it was possible in 1966, it seems to have been tried in the making of&nbsp;<em>Revolver</em>. As Hartley reveals, “I’m Only Sleeping” once featured a vibraphone, “Yellow Submarine” once began with a spoken-word intro, and “And Your Bird Can Sing” once kept getting interrupted by uncontrollable and quite possibly substance-induced laughter. Neither these nor any of the album’s other now-familiar, much-scrutinized songs took straight paths to their final forms. Into a runtime of only 35 minutes, the Beatles managed to put everything from “a children’s singalong song” to “a song complaining about all the money they were paying in taxes” to “a song about loneliness” to “a song about laziness” — while also transmitting the “great feeling of joy” for which their oeuvre is known. <em>Revolver</em>’s place in the canon of albums is secure, but will&nbsp;it still be considered the band’s best work on its&nbsp;70th anniversary? Tomorrow, as they say, never knows.</p>
